The '''Dagaalka Buran''' ([[Burawi language|Burawi]]: "War of the Buran") was a series of military campaigns conducted by the [[Kingdom of Ufyen]] under the late [[Geedi Dynasty]] between approximately 700 and 650 BCE. The campaigns extended Ufyen control down the full length of the [[Buran River]] from the [[Lake Haro]] basin to the [[Mesogean Sea]] coast, bringing the lowland farming communities and coastal fishing settlements of the lower valley under highland tributary rule. The Dagaalka Buran represented the furthest extent of Geedi territorial expansion and established the infrastructure — particularly the ''kaas'' garrison network — that sustained Ufyen's control over the Buran corridor for the next century.

== Background ==

By approximately 700 BCE, the [[Kingdom of Ufyen]] under the [[Geedi Dynasty]] controlled the [[Lake Haro]] basin, the [[Kushish]] iron mines, and the Upper [[Buran River]] valley. The kingdom's capital at [[Badalga-Jir]] and its commercial gateway at [[Jowhar]] were well established, and the ''kaas'' network of fortified hilltop garrisons secured the [[Great Kaftian Mountain Range|mountain passes]] connecting the southern highlands to the [[Northern Basin]].

The Buran River, flowing south from [[Lake Haro]] to the [[Mesogean Sea]], was the peninsula's principal transport route for iron, copper, and timber. The upper reaches of the river were under direct Ufyen control, but the middle and lower valley — a fertile lowland corridor supporting independent farming chiefdoms — remained outside the kingdom's authority. The coastal settlements at and near the river's mouth, including the proto-urban sites that would later develop into [[Republic of Mazar|Mazar]] and [[Kingdom of Iskhal|Iskhal]], traded with the highlands but owed no tribute to the Geedi kings.

Control of the entire Buran corridor would give Ufyen a direct outlet to the sea and eliminate the middlemen who extracted tolls on goods passing through the lower valley.

== Course of the campaigns ==

=== Upper valley consolidation (~700–690 BCE) ===

The initial phase of the Dagaalka Buran involved the consolidation of Ufyen's existing control over the Upper Buran. The Geedi kings strengthened the garrison at [[Jowhar]] and constructed new ''kaas'' forts at key points along the river between Jowhar and the edge of the lowlands. These forts served as staging points for the southward advance and as customs posts for the taxation of river traffic.

=== Middle valley campaign (~690–670 BCE) ===

The main military effort was directed at the independent chiefdoms of the middle Buran valley. These communities, which subsisted on agriculture and river fishing, lacked the iron weapons and fortifications of the highland forces. The Geedi army advanced downstream in stages, establishing a ''kaas'' fort at each major bend or tributary junction before proceeding further south. Resistance was localised: individual chiefdoms fought to defend their territories, but no coordinated alliance formed among the lowland communities.

The middle valley campaign also brought the first significant contact between Ufyen forces and the [[Hiddid]] remnant populations of the lower hills flanking the river corridor. These communities were incorporated as tributary subjects, with their labour directed toward the construction of roads and fortifications.

=== Coastal conquest (~670–650 BCE) ===

The final phase extended Ufyen authority to the [[Mesogean Sea]] coast. The lowland and coastal settlements, including the fishing villages near the Buran mouth and the communities to the southwest that would later become [[Republic of Mazar|Mazar]] and [[Kingdom of Iskhal|Iskhal]], were brought under tributary rule. The Geedi administration did not establish direct governance over the coast; instead, it imposed tribute obligations — payable in fish, salt, and labour — and stationed small ''kaas'' garrisons at the river mouth and at points along the coastal plain.

The coastal settlements retained their internal governance structures and continued their existing patterns of subsistence fishing and local trade. The Geedi presence on the coast was primarily extractive, focused on securing tribute and maintaining a military presence rather than transforming the local economy.

== Aftermath ==

=== The kaas network ===

The Dagaalka Buran resulted in the extension of the ''kaas'' garrison system across the entire length of the Buran valley. By approximately 650 BCE, a chain of fortified hilltop positions connected [[Jowhar]] to the coast, spaced at intervals of roughly one to two days' march. Each ''kaas'' was garrisoned by a small force of highland soldiers under a commander appointed by the Geedi court. The garrisons collected tolls on river traffic, enforced tribute obligations, and served as the first line of defence against raids from the [[Bariites]] to the east and the [[Dakari people|Dakari]] to the north.

=== Economic consequences ===

Control of the full Buran corridor gave the Geedi kings a direct route from the [[Kushish]] iron mines to the coast. This eliminated the tolls previously extracted by the independent middle-valley chiefdoms and increased the volume of iron, copper, and timber reaching the coastal settlements. The resulting growth in coastal trade — though modest compared to what followed [[Aetolians|Aetolian]] contact a generation later — laid the foundations for the development of the settlements that would become the [[Ancient Burawi city-states]].

=== Overextension ===

The Dagaalka Buran also marked the point at which the Ufyen empire reached the limits of its administrative capacity. The kingdom now stretched from the [[Kushish]] highlands to the sea, encompassing highland miners, lowland farmers, [[Hiddid]] hill communities, and coastal fishers — populations with little in common beyond their shared subjection to Geedi tribute demands. The costs of maintaining the extended ''kaas'' network, combined with the difficulty of governing such diverse populations from the highland capital, contributed to the pressures that led to the [[Ancient Southwestern Kaftia#Geedi collapse|Geedi collapse]] approximately a century later.

== Historiography ==

No contemporary written records of the Dagaalka Buran survive; the [[Mesogean script|Echian script]] was not adopted on the peninsula until approximately 600 BCE, after the campaigns had concluded. Knowledge of the war derives from two sources: [[Dalmar Dynasty]]-era stone inscriptions at [[Badalga-Jir]], which refer to the Geedi conquest of "the river from source to sea"; and archaeological evidence, including the remains of ''kaas'' forts along the Buran valley, whose construction techniques and associated pottery are consistent with a late Geedi date.

The oral traditions of the ''[[Northern Kaftian Tradition#Oral tradition|gewel]]'' bardic caste preserve no account of the Dagaalka Buran, as the campaigns took place entirely within the southern peninsula and did not involve the [[Northern Basin]]. The name ''Dagaalka Buran'' ("War of the Buran") is itself a later coinage, first attested in medieval Burawi historiography.

